      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184289",
      ":question/problem": "Prove that if $n$ is an odd integer, then $7n+4$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $7n+4$ is {{field-7}}."
    }
